+---
+id: task-003-audit-applypatch-against-rfc-6902-and-pin-its-conformance
+type: task
+title: "Audit applyPatch against RFC 6902 and pin its conformance"
+status: pending
+---
+
+# Audit `applyPatch` against RFC 6902 and pin its conformance
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: patches they consider invalid being accepted, edge
+cases giving results they did not expect, and failures not behaving the way they read the standard.
+Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to RFC 6902 in every
+case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+The reported symptoms were treated as a conformance question and answered with evidence rather than
+with a guess at which line was wrong. **No behavioural defect was found: `applyPatch` already
+conforms to RFC 6902, in its operations and in the conditions under which it fails.** The source of
+`src/json-patch.ts` is therefore unchanged. What the audit did produce is the two things that were
+genuinely missing — tests pinning the corners nothing covered, and a README paragraph that
+described the behaviour wrongly.
+
+### How conformance was established
+
+Five independent checks, each able to fail on its own:
+
+1. **RFC 6902 appendix A** — every example in the specification, already in the suite (A.1–A.16),
+   re-read against the published text of the RFC rather than from memory.
+2. **The canonical `json-patch-tests` suite** (the vectors at `jsonpatch.com`, Apache-2.0): 91 of 91
+   cases pass, counting the records that carry neither `expected` nor `error` and so must leave the
+   document unchanged.
+3. **Differential testing against three independent implementations** — `json8-patch`, `rfc6902` and
+   `fast-json-patch` — over more than 100 000 randomly generated multi-operation patches, with
+   documents and pointers that included escaped tokens (`~0`, `~1`), members named `__proto__`,
+   `toString`, `a/b` and `a~b`, and root (`""`) paths. Every divergence found was a case where a
+   reference is the lax one and `applyPatch` is right: `rfc6902` cannot patch a document root at
+   all, `fast-json-patch` accepts a `move` whose destination index is past the end of the array it
+   has just shortened, and `json8-patch` lets a `copy` whose `from` location does not exist pass as
+   a silent no-op, where § 4.5 requires it to exist.
+4. **An implementation written from scratch off the RFC text**, kept deliberately naive (and
+   deriving `replace` from § 4.3's "remove followed by add" rather than from a direct write), then
+   diffed exhaustively: 63 882 single-operation and 163 056 two-operation cases over a fixed
+   universe of documents and pointers. Zero differences — in outcome *and* in which of the three
+   failure modes was reported.
+5. **Property checks** over 30 000 generated cases: the document and the patch are never mutated,
+   and no object or array of either is ever reachable from the result.
+
+Two further checks of the checking itself: a cross-product of 1 755 operation shapes confirming that
+each of the six operations requires exactly the members RFC 6902 § 4 says it does and ignores every
+other one however malformed; and `node --test --experimental-test-coverage`, which leaves only three
+defensive `throw error` re-throws uncovered.
+
+### Where the surprise actually comes from
+
+Every symptom in the request maps to a place where the RFC says something that is easy to read the
+other way, and where this implementation follows the RFC:
+
+- *"patches they consider invalid are accepted"* — § 4 requires that members not defined for an
+  operation "MUST be ignored", so an `add` carrying a `from` is valid and the `from` is not even
+  parsed. Likewise `move` from a location to itself, and `copy` into a child of its own source,
+  which only `move` is forbidden (§ 4.4) to do.
+- *"edge cases give results they did not expect"* — § 4.4 makes `move` a `remove` followed by an
+  `add`, so a destination index inside the same array is counted after the element has gone:
+  `/0` to `/2` in `[1,2,3]` gives `[2,3,1]`, and `/0` to `/3` fails. Appendix A.7 is this exact
+  case. On an object, `-` and `0` are plain member names, not indices.
+- *"a patch that fails does not behave the way they read the standard"* — a `test` whose location
+  holds nothing reports `test-failed`, not an operation error: § 4.6 makes success mean "the target
+  location is equal to the value", and § 5 lists "`test` operation failure" among the error
+  conditions. A caller distinguishing a lost race from a bad patch depends on that.
+
+The only behaviour in the library that RFC 6902 does not settle one way or the other is the
+rejection of `remove` with `path: ""` and of `move` with `from: ""`. The RFC has no notion of an
+absent document and `applyPatch` returns a `JsonValue`, so there is nothing to return; the
+alternatives in the wild are `undefined` (`json8-patch`) and `null` (`fast-json-patch`), either of
+which turns a lost document into a present one. The existing choice — reject, as `invalid-patch` —
+was kept and is now documented as a choice rather than as something the RFC requires.
+
+### Changes
+
+- `test/json-patch.test.ts` — seven characterization tests (the behaviour exists; they were expected
+  to pass on their first run, and did), covering what the suite left unpinned: every token being a
+  member name on an object (`-`, `0`, `01`, `""`); only the § 4 array-index form naming an element on
+  an array, across all five operations that reach one (`00`, `01`, `1e0`, `+1`, `-0`, `" 1"`, `1.0`,
+  `0x1`, `length`); escaped tokens decoded in `from` as well as in `path`, with `~01` decoding to
+  `~1` and not to `/`; `move` and `copy` targeting the whole document; an undefined member being
+  ignored even when malformed; a patch being unable to reach an object prototype through
+  `/__proto__/...` or `/constructor/prototype/...`; and a `move` whose `add` half fails after its
+  `remove` half succeeded leaving the document whole.
+- `README.md` — the paragraph on the rejected operations said both of them used `path: ""`, when
+  `move` is rejected on its `from`. Rewritten to name the three rejections correctly, to separate
+  the one § 4.4 mandates from the two the RFC leaves open, and to say why the open ones are
+  rejected. Two bullets gained the facts the new tests pin: that an ignored member is not validated
+  either, and that a pointer reaches `__proto__` only where the document declares it.
+- `src/` — unchanged.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 100 tests, 100 pass, 0 fail (93 before, 7 added).
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, sources and tests, `strict` on.
+- `npm run build` — clean; the rebuilt `dist/` is byte-identical to the committed one, confirming no
+  source changed.
+- The external checks above were re-run after the edits: 91/91 canonical cases, 63 882 exhaustive
+  single-operation cases with 0 differences, 0 problems in the operation-shape cross-product.
+- The README's worked example and the JSON Pointer example above it were run as written and print
+  what they claim.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+None. No new decision was taken: the audit confirms the behaviour `dl-002` already describes, and
+the one point it touches — rejecting `remove`/`move` of the whole document — is that document's
+decision, now also stated in the README as a choice rather than an RFC requirement.
+
+## Follow-up
+
+- `dl-002-shape-and-semantics-of-applypatch-errors-immutability-and-rfc-6902-strictness` and
+  `task-002-implement-json-patch-applypatch-rfc-6902` are both still `pending`. The behaviour they
+  describe is now independently verified, which is the evidence an approver would want.
+- The canonical `json-patch-tests` vectors are Apache-2.0 and were used from a scratch directory,
+  not vendored. If the team wants them run in CI, that is a dev dependency and an attribution to
+  add deliberately, not a side effect of this audit.
+- Equality of numbers is JavaScript's, so two integers beyond 2^53 that `JSON.parse` has rounded to
+  the same double compare equal under `test`. That is a property of `JsonValue` being built on JS
+  numbers, not of the patch code, and it would have to be answered for the whole library at once.
